  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The central idea is to employ a multi-resolution deep Boltzmann machine (DBM) framework optimized with stochastic gradient descent. This differs from previous approaches by (1) using probabilistic representation to capture complex dependencies, (2) enabling hierarchical feature learning across resolutions, and (3) incorporating physics-informed priors for better boundary handling. The DBM structure allows escaping local optima by embedding hierarchical representations, while stochastic weight perturbations help explore rugged energy landscapes. This approach directly minimizes the relaxation of the overlap integral under constraints, offering both global search capabilities and efficient local refinement.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Domain: Piecewise-constant step function on [0,2] sampled at `n_points` points\n   - Objective: Minimize `C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx` (normalized by `dx`)\n   - Constraints: `\u222b h(x)dx = 1`, `0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1`\n   - Representation: \n     h(x) = \u03a3_{i=1}^K w_{\u03c3(i)} * \u03c6_i(x) \n     where S={\u03c6_i} is set of basis functions (piecewise linear/constant), and w weights\n   - Energy formulation:\n     Energy = \u03bb\u2081 * (integral_overlap_max) + \u03bb\u2082 * (||integral-1||\u00b2) + \u03bb\u2083 * (surface_regularization)\n\n   The algorithm formulates this as policy optimization in latent space, with:\n     D_KL( h (latent) || tanh(squashed(logit)) )\n   And energy landscape defined by continuous relaxation of constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - Input: `seed`, budget time limit\n   - Steps:\n     1. Validate input: parse `initial_h_values` if provided for seeding\n     2. Setup hierarchical resolution: n_low=64, n_high=1024, transition at n=512\n     3. Phase 1: Multi-resolution Boltzmann sampling:\n        a. Define basis functions for each resolution scale (j) via adaptive wavelet parametrization:\n           \u03c6_{j,i}^{(h)}(x) = 1 if x in [neighborhood of i for level j], else 0\n        b. Stochastic gradient update using REINFORCE with control variates:\n           param update: \u03b8 \u2190 \u03b8 + \u03b1 * \u2207_\u03b8 [log \u03c0(\u03b8) * (E_withdrawal)]\n        c. Energy-scoring of samples using physics-informed entropy minimization\n     4. Phase 2: Gibbs sampling projection:\n        a. MCMC moves restricted to constraint manifold via adaptive rejection sampling\n        b. Use No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) for HMC with natural gradient\n        c. Maintain temperature schedule T(t) = 1 / (1 + t^\u03b2)\n     5. Phase 3: Local refinement with projection descent:\n        a. Solve constrained optimization: minimize_C5(h) subject to ||\u222bh||\u22481 and [0,1] \n        b. Use projected gradient with curvature estimation\n     6. Placeholder function (requires full implementation):\n        def physics_informed_prior(x, scale):\n            \"\"\"Return log-energy incorporating physical weights\"\"\"\n            term ... \n\n   - Candidate generation: \n        candidates = {}\n        for n in [64,256,512,1024]:\n            use_reinforce(n, initial_temp=1.0)\n\n   4. Search choices and variations\n   - Default parameters:\n        n_phases = 3\n        n_phases_iterations = [10, 50, 100]\n        temp_schedule = lambda t: t**(-0.3)\n        temperatures_schedule = [3.0, 0.5, 0.1]\n        basis_functions = ['sigmoid', 'piecewise_linear']\n        runner_percentage = 0.75   # fraction of time running active search\n        budget allocator = [300 seconds for each resolution]\n\n   - Variations:\n        - Resolution variation: If no improvement at n=512, double grid size to 2048 (with timeout check)\n        - prior adjustment: Weight boundary terms higher (\u03bb\u2083 increased by 0.2) for smoother transitions\n        - gradient clipping: \u03bb\u2082 above 10 for more exploration\n\n   5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Estimation:\n        For n=1024: 200,000 operations for correlation (O(n\u00b2)), so 200,000 * 0.0005 sec ~= 100 seconds per iteration (using operations estimated at 1e7 per second)\n        Total iterations: 40 (average)\n        Global processing time estimate: 4000 seconds -> 66.7 minutes well within budget\n\n   - Robustness measures:\n        Timeout handler after 2/3 budget\n        Fallback to previous best solution if NaN/Inf occurs\n        Implement energy stability checks: ensure [0,1] projection and sum \u2192 budgetially compensated by adaptive step sizes\n\n   6. Validation and return contract\n   - Final solution must pass: \n        - 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 for all i\n        - \u222bh dx = 1 (absolute tolerance |error| < 1e-4)\n        - Objective evaluation via: np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')) * dx \n        - Field validation of C5 at original resolution via interpolation\n   - Return structure: (optimized_h, c5_value, n_points)\n   - If timeout: return best_valid_candidate found during execution\n   - Conditional startup: requires to seed from parent if available and valid",
